diff --git a/docs/reference/packing.md b/docs/reference/packing.md
index 1842e43..60d6f91 100644
--- a/docs/reference/packing.md
+++ b/docs/reference/packing.md
@@ -797,3 +797,10 @@
 - **Fix:** `uepak.py` tries 20-byte hashes first and switches to 8 only if the tables fail to parse with matching counts and consume the whole chunk. The version 2 archive stores each 8-byte hash with 12 zero bytes appended. Version 12 follows version 11's footer rules. For untagged Switch records, `raw_record` looks for a unique control section with matching code lengths and a `0x880`-byte control length. `shader_split.ue_shaders` takes the stage from the archive's frequency for those records.
 - **Check:** `test_uepak_iostore.py` covers both hash widths, a wrong-width parse that reaches the chunk's end, valid and legacy-position version 12 footers, and untagged records with code or control length mismatches or a copy of the control magic inside shader code. The [PR author](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/3022) built 6,878 Mac shaders for update 131072.
 - **Found on:** Godzilla: Destroy All Monsters Melee Remastered (`0100A00026D70000`).
+
+### Programs added by updates
+
+- **Symptom:** extracting a secondary program fails with `no Program NCA ... in the base NSP`, although the update contains that program. Packing only program 0 produces a launcher without the playable programs.
+- **Cause:** an update may add a program absent from the base package. Its data may be complete or may use a relocation table over another base program's data. Matching only the Program NCA title ID misses this case.
+- **Fix:** `nx_extract` accepts a non-sparse added program with complete sections directly. For a patched data section, it tries the base package's Program NCAs and reads the entire combined data through integrity verification before selecting one. A program without a compatible base still fails. Control and HtmlDocument selection retain the requested program ID. The collection profile lists its child profiles through `programs`.
+- **Found on:** [XCOM 2 Collection](../../dm/games/0100D0B00FB74000/README.md), package `v131072`, program 2. Its full patched data and extracted manifest passed integrity checks. Gameplay and handoff remain untested.
diff --git a/docs/reference/shaders.md b/docs/reference/shaders.md
index 1c1adb3..9ad7589 100644
--- a/docs/reference/shaders.md
+++ b/docs/reference/shaders.md
@@ -3334,3 +3334,11 @@ Finding, keying and translating shaders (`dm/gfx/tools/shader_split.py`, `dmsh_b
 - **Fix:** `bit0_mask` in `dmsh_rewrite.py` (run by `dmsh_build.py` on every shader, a geometry shader's kernel and its reader too): the mask is written from the bit, `-(x & 1)`, the same value, and the compare is left with its branch. It rewrites only `= b ? (-1) : 0;` where `b` is declared as `a != 0` and `a` as `… & 1`, neither assigned again, within one function; a shader without that comes back unchanged. Other bits and other uses of the compare (a select of two constants, a conversion) did not show the bug in the reduced shader, and are left as the translator wrote them.
 - **Found on:** [PRAGMATA](../../dm/games/0400C76024710000/README.md): pixel shader `c4ac3ca598cea32e` (`/re_chunk_000.pak#e1487969619ddb7a#SDF#var15`) tests bit 0 of a constant buffer word (`fp_c10[2].w`) after 6 texture reads and a discard, keeps the predicate and branches on it twice. The front end's EarlyCSE, which merges the repeated loads of that word, is what leaves the compare with its two users ([A base the GPU compiler cannot encode cost the whole offline archive](#a-base-the-gpu-compiler-cannot-encode-cost-the-whole-offline-archive), which keeps such a pipeline out of the offline archive). Reduced with a line-level delta debugger against `applegpu-nt` to the 100-line shader of the test; the failing instruction was read with a debugger on a copy of `applegpu-nt` (a conditional jump whose operand is a uniform register, emitted once, as the last instruction before the abort). M4 Pro, macOS 26.6.2, Metal toolchain 32023.922.
 - **Check:** `python3 dm/gfx/tests/test_dmsh_bit0_mask.py` (the rewrite, what it leaves alone, each function by itself). With `DMSH_TEST_METAL=1` the reduced shader goes through `metal` with the chain's flags and `applegpu-nt`: as translated it must fail with `Failed to encode` (the test is skipped where a later toolchain compiles it), rewritten it must compile at `-O1` and `-O2` for `applegpu_g15g`, `g16s` and `g17g`.
+
+### Unreal shared control and code arrays
+
+- **Symptom:** the shader split decodes every Unreal Engine 3 package but reports zero shaders. A `UniShaderBuffer` package holds NVN control and code magics in separate regions.
+- **Cause:** the shared shader buffer serializes a byte-counted array of control sections and reflection, then a byte-counted array of code areas in the same order. The ordinary UE3 reader requires each code area immediately before its control section.
+- **Fix:** `ue3_shared_shaders` recognizes the shared-buffer class, bounds both arrays, pairs each control with the next code area using control `+0x704`, and validates the stage and code length. The controls' own lengths exclude intervening reflection bytes. Headerless compute stages use their control's thread-group dimensions. The shaders must consume the entire code array.
+- **Check:** `test_ue3_cache.py` covers the separate arrays, intervening reflection and truncated or unconsumed code. The program 1 shared cache from XCOM 2 contains 33,034 records: 20,963 pixel, 12,062 vertex, five compute and four geometry, all passing stage and length checks.
+- **Found on:** [XCOM 2 Collection](../../dm/games/0100D0B00FB74000/README.md), package `v131072`. This validates extraction, not translated rendering or gameplay.
